= Persistent state contamination (artificial intelligence) =

== Overview ==
'''Persistent state contamination''' is a condition in which an erroneous, misleading, misattributed, stale, or malicious inference or action result is written into durable agent state and later reused as if it were trustworthy context.

The defining transition is:

<pre>
transient error or hostile input
→ durable write
→ later retrieval
→ renewed influence
</pre>

Persistence changes the failure from a single-run mistake into a state-management problem.

== What counts as persistent state? ==
Persistent state can include:

* long-term agent memory;
* user or agent profiles;
* knowledge graphs;
* task histories;
* summaries;
* checkpoints;
* plans and lessons learned;
* preferences;
* tool-derived records;
* configuration generated or modified by the agent.

The medium is not important. The key property is survival beyond the local step or execution that produced the state.

== Contamination versus memory poisoning ==
'''Memory poisoning''' is an increasingly studied adversarial attack in which an attacker causes malicious or fabricated content to be stored in an agent's long-term memory and later activated through retrieval.

Persistent state contamination is broader.

It includes memory poisoning, but can also arise accidentally through:

* hallucinated facts written as memory;
* incorrect summaries;
* stale records;
* failed tool results stored as successes;
* state written under the wrong identity or scope;
* mistaken inference promoted into canonical configuration;
* corrupted checkpoint restoration.

Thus:

<pre>
memory poisoning ⊂ adversarial persistent-state contamination
persistent-state contamination also includes non-adversarial failures
</pre>

== Why persistence changes the risk ==
A transient error can disappear when the context ends.

A durable error can:

* cross session boundaries;
* be retrieved repeatedly;
* appear more credible because it is "remembered";
* influence future plans or tool calls;
* be summarized into new records;
* spread to shared or multi-agent memory;
* survive model replacement or process restart.

This gives persistent state a different failure geometry from ordinary prompt-level error.

== Write, store, retrieve, act ==
A useful lifecycle is:

<pre>
input/event
→ proposed memory write
→ storage
→ retrieval
→ interpretation
→ action
→ possible new write
</pre>

Contamination can enter at any write and then propagate through later phases.

Research on long-term memory security therefore increasingly treats memory governance as a lifecycle problem rather than only a retrieval filter.

== Provenance and authority ==
[[Provenance]] can record:

* source;
* generating activity;
* responsible agent;
* derivation history;
* time/version;
* transformations.

Provenance does not make a claim true, but it allows later systems to avoid treating all stored state as equally authoritative.

Recent memory-security research goes further by arguing for origin-bound authority, write-time controls, versioning, quarantine, and rollback because an agent's own summarization can otherwise launder untrusted content into apparently trusted memory.

== Relationship to state misbinding ==
[[State misbinding (artificial intelligence)|State misbinding]] can contaminate persistent state even when the source record is correct.

For example:

<pre>
Agent A's correct state
→ written into Agent B's namespace
→ later retrieved by B as self-state
</pre>

The content was not originally false. The persistent store became contaminated through incorrect binding.

== Relationship to error compounding ==
Once contaminated state is retrieved, it changes future inputs.

This creates a pathway to [[Error compounding in sequential systems|sequential error compounding]]:

<pre>
bad durable state
→ biased future decision
→ bad result
→ result stored as additional state
→ stronger future bias
</pre>

The loop can also be corrected if later evidence, validation, rollback, or negative feedback invalidates the bad state.

== Controls ==
Useful controls include:

* write-time provenance;
* source/authority labels;
* explicit identity and scope binding;
* validation before durable writes;
* confidence and uncertainty retention;
* contradiction detection;
* quarantine/review states;
* immutable history plus reversible derived views;
* versioning and rollback;
* memory expiration/forgetting;
* retrieval-time policy;
* periodic re-audit of durable state.

The correct combination depends on threat model and architecture.

== Epistemic notes ==
Persistent-memory poisoning is now directly studied in agent-security literature. "Persistent state contamination" is a broader BITwiki synthesis intended to include both adversarial poisoning and accidental durable-state corruption. It should not be cited as if it were already a standardized security taxonomy.

Several 2026 results are recent preprints. They provide direct evidence that writable cross-session memory can preserve and reactivate malicious state, but individual attack-success rates should not be generalized beyond the tested systems.

